## Expert Summary

The marquise cut has one of the oldest histories in diamond cutting. King Louis XV of France commissioned the shape in the 18th century, reportedly inspired by the lips of his mistress, the Marquise de Pompadour. The name stuck.

The shape survived every trend cycle that followed, including the post-80s collapse of its popularity, and has come back strongly in the 2020s as buyers seek distinctive silhouettes.

Its revival is driven by one thing: it is the most elongating diamond shape available.

At a standard 2.00 L:W ratio, a 1ct marquise measures approximately 10.5mm tip-to-tip. A 1ct round brilliant measures 6.4mm across. A 1ct emerald cut measures approximately 7.0mm along its longest axis.

The marquise stretches further than any of them.

The cost of that length is double bow tie risk and double tip vulnerability.

Every marquise diamond has two pointed tips. Both require V-prong protection. And the elongated middle section of the marquise creates a bow tie shadow — the same optical phenomenon that affects pear and oval cuts — that must be evaluated via video before purchasing.

Unlike a pear with one pointed tip, the marquise has two: each tip can chip, and each wing can display a bow tie.

The emerald cut has none of these vulnerabilities.

Its cropped corners are far more durable than pointed tips. Its step-cut facets produce no bow tie — the hall-of-mirrors effect is immune to the optical geometry that creates bow tie in elongated brilliant cuts.

The trade-off is that the emerald cut shows inclusions and body color far more clearly than the marquise, requiring higher clarity and color grades to achieve the same visual result. A VS2 marquise looks eye-clean. A VS2 emerald often doesn't.

A G color marquise looks white. A G color emerald is the minimum — anything warmer shows in the open table. The clarity and color premium on the emerald adds real cost to the budget calculation.

Natural emerald 1ct GIA D-VS1 starts at [$3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). Marquise at comparable grades runs in a similar range at 1ct but allows lower practical clarity — a G-VS2 marquise looks eye-clean, while a G-VS2 emerald shows inclusions.

---

## Shape Geometry: How Marquise and Emerald Cut Are Built

The marquise cut is an elongated brilliant cut derived from the round brilliant. Its outline is a pointed oval — two symmetrical arcs meeting at pointed tips at both ends. The standard marquise has 58 facets arranged to maximize brilliance and fire across the long body of the stone.

It is the most elongated standard brilliant cut in common use.

The marquise's length-to-width ratio determines how dramatically it elongates — ratios from 1.85 to 2.15 are the accepted ideal, with 2.00 being the most popular. A shorter marquise (1.75 or below) begins to look stubby and loses the characteristic elongating effect that makes buyers choose it. A longer marquise (above 2.20) can look spindly and emphasizes bow tie more severely.

The emerald cut is a step cut with a rectangular outline and cropped corners. Its 57–58 facets are arranged in long parallel rows on the crown and pavilion, creating a completely different optical behavior from the brilliant-cut marquise. Light enters through the large flat table and reflects between the step facets in slow, dramatic planes.

The two shapes have almost nothing in common except that both are longer than they are wide.

