## The Double Bow Tie

The bow tie is the most discussed risk in fancy-shape brilliant diamonds.

The marquise has two of them.

A bow tie is a dark optical shadow that forms across the middle of elongated brilliant cuts — marquise, pear, and oval — because light entering the elongated section exits the pavilion at angles the eye reads as darkness. In a pear cut, there is one tip, so the bow tie shadow forms as a single band. In a marquise, both ends are pointed, so the optical conditions for bow tie exist at both wings simultaneously.

The result is The Double Bow Tie.

In a well-cut marquise with a light bow tie, this shadow is minimal and barely noticeable in most lighting. The brilliant facets on either side of the bow tie are active and bright, drawing the eye away from the center darkness.

In a poorly-cut marquise with a severe double bow tie, the center of the stone looks like a dark, dead band stretching across the entire width of the diamond. It is highly visible and significantly detracts from the stone's beauty.

> **Farzana says:** "The double bow tie is the reason I always say: never buy a marquise diamond without watching a video of that specific stone. I've seen buyers receive marquise diamonds expecting brilliance and find a dark stripe across the middle instead. The photos on any website will never show it accurately. Video is the only way."

**Double bow tie severity guide:**

<table class="dc-custom-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Severity</th>
<th>Appearance</th>
<th>Acceptable?</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>None / Faint</td><td>No visible shadow in normal lighting</td><td>Yes — ideal, rare</td></tr>
<tr><td>Light</td><td>Soft shadow visible in direct overhead light only</td><td>Yes — acceptable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moderate</td><td>Clearly visible band across center, detracts from brilliance</td><td>Borderline — evaluate carefully</td></tr>
<tr><td>Severe</td><td>Dark, dominant stripe visible from any angle</td><td>No — reject this stone</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Double bow tie severity is invisible on a GIA or IGI certificate. It cannot be calculated from cut data. The only method of assessment is video of the specific stone under mixed natural and artificial lighting.

The emerald cut has no bow tie of any kind. Its step-cut geometry does not produce the optical conditions that create bow tie. This makes emerald cut purchasing simpler in one respect — you do not need to evaluate bow tie before buying.

---

## The Finger Effect

Both shapes elongate the finger. The degree, method, and visual impact differ significantly.

The marquise achieves elongation through extreme length. At a 2.00 L:W ratio, a 1ct marquise stretches nearly 10.5mm along the finger — longer than a 1ct round, oval, pear, or emerald cut. The two pointed tips anchor visually at each end of the stone, drawing the eye along the full length of the diamond and extending the perceived length of the finger beneath it.

No other shape elongates as dramatically as the marquise at equal carat weight.

The emerald cut achieves elongation through clean rectangular geometry. Its length reads as sophisticated extension rather than dramatic sweep. A 1.45 L:W emerald at 1ct measures approximately 7.5×5.2mm — shorter than a marquise but creating a sleek, Art Deco elongation that many buyers prefer for its restraint.

> **Farzana says:** "The marquise is the shape for buyers who want people to notice the ring from across the room. The emerald is for buyers who want people to notice the stone when they look closer. Both are beautiful. They're doing completely different jobs."

