## Color: How Each Shape Reads

The marquise has a specific color behavior that differs from round and cushion brilliant cuts. The pointed tips of a marquise concentrate color — body warmth collects at the points and reads more intensely there than in the center of the stone.

This is called tip color concentration.

Because of it, marquise cut buyers in white gold or platinum should target G color minimum — the same as emerald cut. H can occasionally work in marquise if the stone has well-proportioned wings and the tips are small relative to the overall body, but H in a marquise with longer, more pointed tips will show warmth at the ends.

Both shapes need G in white gold or platinum. Emerald needs it because the open table shows body color. Marquise needs it because the tips concentrate it.

**Color minimum guide:**

<table class="dc-custom-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metal</th>
<th>Marquise Cut</th>
<th>Emerald Cut</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Platinum</td><td>G minimum (tips show color)</td><td>G minimum</td></tr>
<tr><td>White gold</td><td>G minimum</td><td>G minimum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Yellow gold</td><td>I acceptable (metal neutralizes tips)</td><td>H minimum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rose gold</td><td>H or I</td><td>H minimum</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

In yellow gold settings, marquise has a significant advantage — the warm metal neutralizes color concentration at the tips, allowing buyers to go lower on color without visible warmth. An I color marquise in yellow gold looks indistinguishable from a G color to the naked eye.

