## Proportions and Symmetry: What to Target

Marquise cut proportions are more complex than most other shapes because symmetry is critical in a way it is not for cushion or oval.

The two wings of a marquise must be mirror images. If one wing curves more than the other — or if the tips are not perfectly aligned on the center axis — the stone looks off-balance face-up.

This is called wing asymmetry. It is visible to the naked eye and is one of the most common quality issues in marquise diamonds.

GIA grades overall symmetry as Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, or Poor — but even a GIA "Very Good" symmetry grade does not guarantee the wings look visually balanced face-up. The symmetry grade covers many factors beyond wing matching. Always review face-up photos and video of the specific stone, not just the certificate data.

Always evaluate symmetry in a marquise cut.

L:W ratio target for marquise: 1.85–2.15, with 2.00 being the classic standard. Below 1.75, the stone looks too wide. Above 2.20, the stone becomes too elongated and the bow tie typically worsens.

