## Settings: What Works Best

Marquise cuts have mandatory setting requirements that go beyond most other shapes.

Both pointed tips need V-prongs. Not claws, not single prongs — V-prongs specifically designed to cradle the tip without exposing it. An exposed marquise tip is a chip waiting to happen.

Beyond tip protection, marquise cuts work well in six-prong solitaires (four on the body, one V-prong at each tip), in east-west settings where the stone is rotated 90 degrees, and in vintage-inspired settings with milgrain or engraving that echoes the stone's dramatic outline. Halo settings on marquise cuts are popular and add significant visual presence, but the halo must be custom-fitted to the marquise outline — a generic oval halo will not sit correctly.

Three-stone settings with tapered baguettes or matching marquise side stones pair well with a marquise center.

Emerald cuts look best in settings that highlight their clean geometry. Four-prong solitaires with long, straight prongs, bezel settings, and Art Deco-inspired settings with geometric milgrain all complement the emerald cut. Three-stone settings with baguette side stones are the classic pairing.

> **Farzana says:** "A marquise in a bezel setting is one of the most underrated looks in fine jewelry. The bezel protects both tips completely and gives the stone a modern, clean outline. If you're worried about tip vulnerability, bezel the marquise and forget about it."

